WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.980
3Blue1Brown - "A Curious Pattern Indeed"
[CC by Yuval Dolev]

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:08.720
Pick two points on a circle,
and draw a line straight through.

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:11.800
The space which was encircled
Is divided into two.

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:14.900
To this point add a third one,
which gives us two more chords.

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:18.220
The space through which this lines run,
has been fissured into four.

00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:21.340
Continue with a fourth point,
and three more lines drawn straight,

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:24.700
Now the count of disjoint regions,
sums, in all, to eight.

00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:28.080
A fifth point and its four lines,
support this pattern glean,

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:31.340
Counting sections, one divines,
that there are now sixteen.

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:34.620
This pattern here of doubling,
does seem a sturdy one,

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:37.540
But one more step is troubling,
as the sixth gives thirty-one.

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:39.380
Wait.. what?

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:41.380
1, 2, 4, 8, 16, ...

00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:42.640
31?

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:44.640
What's going on here?

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:47.060
Why does the pattern start off as powers of two,

00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:49.840
Only to fall short by one at the sixth iteration?

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:51.840
That seems arbitrary.

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:55.760
Why not - 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 63?

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:58.200
Or - 1, 2, 4, 8, 15?

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:02.720
If you keep going,  the number of section deviates even further from powers of two,

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:04.720
except when it hits 256,

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:07.840
But this just begs the question of what the pattern really is,

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:09.840
and why it flirts with powers of two.

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:15.200
In my next few videos, I'll explain what's happening, which will include one of my all-time favorite proofs.

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:18.500
But interesting problem deserve to be shared, pondered, and discussed,

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:20.800
Before their secrets are hastily revealed.

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:24.860
So while I work on animating my explanation,
I encourage you to think of your own.

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:27.020
To be clear, the question is this:

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:29.540
If you take some set of points on a circle,

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:31.980
You connect every pair of them with a line,

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:35.020
how many pieces do these lines cut the circle into?

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:37.760
Does it matter where these points are?

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:42.900
And why does the answer coencide with powers of two,
for fewer than six points?

